ring this stage  will be more susceptible to infection  and subsequent contamination so if a  plant experiences drought during this  time then it is more likely to have  mycotoxin at harvest whereas later in  the season after fungal establishment on  the ear rains at maturity are actually  more likely to make a more conducive  environment for fungal growth and result  in more contamination across the season  soil health is very important as it's  low poor soil health such as low  nitrogen low carbon contributes to plant  stress and makes a plant more  susceptible to infection so I'm  interested in how all of these  environmental factors could use to model  micro toxin contamination across region  and so the study site is Congo District  here in central Tanzania and Congo  district is a semi-arid smallholder  farming system we have collaborators  working on a child with a nutrition  trial there and as part of that work  we've established this contamination by  both aflatoxin and fume honest in' and  so I'm just showing here  that's childhood stunting which is what  this trial is looking at is elevated in  Congo and across all of Tanzania so this  is especially relevant in these areas as  even earlier this year a high aflatoxin  contamination of maize has been reported  to cause an outbreak of athletic  psychosis which resulted in eight deaths  in regions bordering Kawa so to try and  get at this question of how the  environments influencing the geographic  dispersion of micro toxins across  smallholder farming system I first went  to Kong WA interviewed farmers and  sampled basic grain mills then  quantified  Mays and aflatoxin values by  enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay next  acquired remote sensing imagery of  different environmental risk factors and  finally tried to model mycotoxins a  function of these remote sensing  indicators so concentration of  mycotoxins as a function of things like  NDVI soil carbon harvest etc so for my  survey I visited Congo district 