I averaged over sort of the distance  someone walked to the grain mill so I  knew their farm was X kilometers away  but I had no in which direction so it's  for someone two kilometres away it would  be a relatively smaller radius versus  eight kilometers would be a larger  radius of average values and you can  also use NDVI to compare to previous  years with this data set and so here at  a hundred percent is the median NDVI of  approximately twenty years of data and  you can see that for the beginning of  the growing season things were more or  less at the median then after that  drought began in the middle of the  season you can see that compared to past  years and dbi plummeted where and then  rains started again late in the season  and you see a sort of rebound effect and  I want to show sort of how this  correlates to our thoughts of drought  affecting during the reproductive growth  period and then rains occurring again at  the end of the growing season I also  looked at  soil health using the soil grids 250  meter data set this has many soil  attributes at high spatial resolution  and in particularly looked at organic  carbon stock which is the amount of  carbon within a given area and see some  greener areas these are like mountains  where there's actually no farming and  there's more trees but across mostly  these white and beige areas that are  very low values are where most of the  farming is going on and so that sort of  is how I'm trying to monitor this other  risk factor of soil health so I use  several different models to try and  model both alpha toxins and human  essence only models for athletics in  were significant based on environmental  factors I ended up choosing a mixed  effects model incorporating both fixed  and random effects a reduced model found  that the fixed effects of NDVI in  april/may and organic carbon stock from  the 15 to 30 centimeter root zone along  with the random effects of each grain  mill so samples were associated with the  grain mill and I used that as my random  effects in doing this I want to focus  first on the estimates for my fixed  effects so in April so nearing the end  of the grain the growing season there's  a negative association between aflatoxin  and nvv I sort of as we'd expect the  more stress crops were had were  negatively associated with alpha toxin  whereas in May after the growing season  there was a positive association shown  here sort of maybe suggesting that areas  that had more rain at the end of the  growing season were more likely to be  contaminated and as other studies have  shown organic carbon was negatively  associated with alpha toxin so in total  the fixed effects explained 11.2% of the  variance in athlete's oxen which is not  a huge number but for such a small area  and a diverse system it is significant  and when I account for my random of  along with fixed effects and conditional  r-squared it's almost 28% I wanted to  see how well this worked for predicting  if I held samples out of my training set  so doing that I could compare predicted  and observed aflatoxins in my test set  and found a strong correlation between  predicted and observed though I should  note that there because of the low  variance explained it's a pretty narrow  range of predicted values so takeaways  from this are that Congo District in  2019 had an extremely tough maize  growing season that resulted in  significant aflatoxin contamination  however it did provide an opportunity to  really understand how these  environmental risk factors that are  freely available and our pre harvest  could be used to create predictive  models obviously such models would need  further validation across locations and  years but could help plant breeders  understand variance in mycotoxin hammock  contamination across regions or even  public health experts who want early  warning systems for these types of  problems with that I really want to  thank many people including everyone in  the Nelson lab our collaborators with  the mycotoxin mitigation trial I  mentioned John I'm stronger my research  assistant all the participants and  people in Congo who made this much  easier than it could have been and  funding from the Atkinson Center and the  an Audi Center so thank you and I we  have time for a few questions for will  yeah yeah yeah you're very correct so he  asked about the difference between pre  harvest and post-harvest contamination  aflatoxins are notorious for  especially post-harvest problems and  there's a lot of research on storage  techniques and processing techniques to  reduce that I wanted to just focus on  the pre-harvest aspect and so actually  that's why I was when I visited it was  directly after harvest so samples for  only days or weeks from being in the  field so I want to sort of isolate that  part and then understanding post harvest  issues it's a whole nother part of it  but yeah yeah so I I did end my  questionnaire have questions about what  seats people were growing and all those  kinds of things and it is there's first  very low understanding of what I mean  people are growing open pollinated saved  seeds this is I think I met one person  out of over 300 who had purchased hybrid  seed or something like that so there are  no inputs so very diverse very diverse  genetics and so that was something I was  interested in trying to parse out but  was ultimately kind of impossible yeah  but genetics are highly quantitative low  heritability for this but interested in  trying to look at that a little more to  one said they did not so 305 out of 306  so that was like a very unanimous like  loud signal to me that it was a big  problem yeah mark in terms of cultural  practices a lot of it has to do with I  mean some say early maturity early  maturity variety  so they get out of the field field  quicker proper drying techniques their  proper storage techniques so even some  trying to use anoxic storage conditions  dry storage conditions this is really  like an issue that has involves so many  so many different factors even sorting  post-harvest but you know in these very  very low input systems it's difficult to  say there's exactly one thing that can  solve it all yeah all right let's think  will this has been a production of  Cornell University on the web at Cornell  edu

